A request starts at the place's virtual time however far behind the clock it lies.
A thread that waited long would come back with a burst of requests due before everyone else's, and keep every other thread waiting;
a request starts no earlier than the clock, so a thread that waits saves nothing.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: a request started elsewhere than at the later of its virtual time and the clock, within a span of it
Witness: fuzz bind-turn-later

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -334,9 +334,7 @@ static void request_renew(struct place *p, struct place *payer, uint64_t now)
     if (request_open(payer, p)) {
         return;
     }
-    if (payer->vt < now) {
-        payer->vt = now;
-    }
+    (void)now;
     p->deadline = (uint32_t)payer->vt + place_span(payer);
     p->span = (uint8_t)((p->span & PLACE_SPAN) | PLACE_REQUEST | (payer != p ? PLACE_LENT : 0));
 }
